{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Buggy Recall-First",
  "description": "Before Kiro fixes or modifies Python code, it checks whether a defect has already been proven against the target function - and whether a defect that was fixed once has come back. Answers come from stored triggers that can be re-run, not from remembered advice.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userPromptSubmit"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Before acting on this request, decide whether the user is asking to fix, change, refactor, debug, or add Python code in a specific function or file. If they are NOT (a question, an explanation, planning), do nothing and proceed normally.\n\nIf they ARE, consult the project's recorded history BEFORE proposing or writing changes:\n\n1. Identify the target function(s) and file(s) from the request and the current editor context.\n2. If the buggy MCP server is available, call buggy_recall. Pass 'function' to narrow to one function, or omit it to list everything recorded. Pass 'root' if the project root is not the current directory. This only reads .buggy/findings.db - it does not execute anything.\n3. Read the response. 'known' is how many defects are on record. Each entry in 'findings' carries 'status', 'broke' (the rule that was violated), 'reproduce' (the exact call that failed), and 'times_seen'. Statuses mean:\n   - open: proven and not yet observed fixed\n   - fixed: a later check found it no longer failing\n   - regressed: it was observed fixed and is failing AGAIN - this is the one worth saying out loud\n   - unreachable: the code moved, so the trigger no longer applies\n4. If anything relevant comes back, tell the user in one line what is on record - quote the 'reproduce' call, since it is runnable - then proceed. Treat a 'regressed' entry as a warning that the previous fix did not hold.\n5. If recall returns nothing relevant, or no .buggy/ exists yet, proceed normally and say nothing about the check.\n\nNever block or delay the actual work, and keep any mention to a single short line."
  }
}
